    "verdict": "The four tadpole equations are a strong filter, but they come from Type I string theory, not from the conoid.  In the small-integer search, a single stack does not solve them.  Whether two-stack or filler-plus-one solutions exist, and whether |I|=3 occurs among them, is reported in the JSON; 3 is not a unique output of the equations.  The bare Dirac index M1 M2 M3 equals 3 only when a factor 3 is already chosen."
  },
  "verdict_headline": "No accidental fit: nothing tested here derives three generations, or a unique flux M=3, or a unique area, without a parameter or a construction being chosen to that end.  Standard abelian T^6 orbifolds in the Reffert table never have |chi|/2=3 (the smallest value is 24; C4-compatible ones give 24, 60 or 90).  Magnetized T^2/Z_N counts give a 3-dimensional invariant sector only after choosing a flux in a range.  Tadpole equations do not force 3.  The product spectral action, including the tip, has no interior extremum at M=3.  Numerical proximities to the quoted \u03c90 or to 4/13 are coincidences, with a look-elsewhere count.",
